      "Node.js — the runtime under a workload that is almost entirely I/O rather than computation. Messaging fan-out, notification delivery, discovery reads and video job orchestration are all waiting on sockets, caches and remote services, which is exactly what a non-blocking event loop handles efficiently: one process holds a very large number of concurrent connections without a thread per connection. It also shares a language with TypeScript across every service, so types and validation logic are written once.",
      "AWS MediaConvert — a managed, job-based transcoding service rather than self-hosted encoders. Video processing is the most expensive and least predictable work in the platform, and the serverless model means capacity is not provisioned for peak upload volume and left idle between bursts. Just as importantly, it makes transcoding asynchronous by construction, so the upload path returns immediately and encoder throughput never becomes user-facing request latency.",
      "Socket.io — the real-time transport layer, chosen because raw WebSockets are only part of the problem. It handles transport negotiation and fallback where WebSockets are blocked by intermediaries, plus heartbeats and automatic reconnection, which on the mobile networks a local-discovery product runs on are the normal case rather than an edge case. That removes a large amount of connection-lifecycle code from the application itself.",
